Chapter: ProbeRecursiveEq
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Structural equality on a SELF-RECURSIVE sum, which Update 52 turned from a
 compile-time refusal into working code.

 Before Update 52 the x86 back end answered `==` on a sum containing itself
 with `cdx-recursive-structural-eq`: "This backend compares a sum by inlining
 a field compare", and inlining has no correct bound, so any depth limit
 compares only part of the value. Update 52 replaced the refusal with a
 synthesised `__eq_<Sum>` helper per recursive sum the program actually
 compares, called rather than inlined, so the recursion terminates at runtime
 on the data.

 That is why no tier covered this until now: it could not be written down. A
 program asking the question did not compile.

 The zig plug has no structural path at all. `emit-zig-binary`'s IrEq arm
 (ZigEmitter.codex) special-cases Text and otherwise emits raw zig `==`. In
 that plug every record is a POINTER -- finding 10, all of them, not just the
 ones spelled `mutable` -- so `==` on a composite is either identity or a zig
 compile error, depending on the representation the sum got. Bare metal now
 compares CONTENTS. Whichever way the zig arm answers, this file is where the
 two stop agreeing.

 EVERY VALUE IS BUILT THROUGH A FUNCTION, and that is not decoration. Tier 9
 learned it the expensive way: a one-line wrapper let the inliner collapse two
 constructions into `cx_address_of(c) == cx_address_of(c)`, a tautology that
 would have passed on any arm including a broken one. Two trees written as
 literals side by side invite the same folding, and a constant-folded `True`
 answers the question without ever running the comparison.

 The different-shape rows are the control. An arm comparing pointers answers
 "no" to every row here, including the ones that should be yes -- so a file
 with only equal-shape rows could not tell a pointer comparison from a broken
 structural one. The self-comparison row is the second control: it is the one
 row a pointer comparison gets RIGHT, so an arm answering yes/no/yes has been
 caught doing identity rather than structure.

 Expected on bare metal under Update 52: yes, no, yes, yes, no.

Section: Types

  Tree =
    | Leaf (Integer)
    | Fork (Tree) (Tree)

Section: Instrument

  show-yn : Boolean -> Text
  show-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: Shapes

  small : Integer -> Tree
  small (n) = Fork (Leaf n) (Leaf (n + 1))

  deep : Integer -> Tree
  deep (n) = Fork (small n) (Fork (Leaf n) (small (n + 2)))

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni "-- one level of recursion --"
   let a = small 1
   in let b = small 1
   in let c = small 9
   in act
     print-line-uni ("equal shape, two objects   : " & show-yn (a == b))
     print-line-uni ("different shape            : " & show-yn (a == c))
     print-line-uni ("a value compared to itself : " & show-yn (a == a))
   end
   print-line-uni "-- nested, so a depth-one compare cannot pass by luck --"
   let d = deep 3
   in let e = deep 3
   in let f = deep 4
   in act
     print-line-uni ("equal shape, two objects   : " & show-yn (d == e))
     print-line-uni ("different shape            : " & show-yn (d == f))
   end
  end
